WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.509
In the penultimate episode of Star Trek: Picard's
first season, La Sirena finally reaches its

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:10.770
destination, Soji learns the truth of her
origins, and Picard sees an unexpected familiar

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:11.260
face.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
Here are all the Easter eggs and references
you might have missed in "Et in Arcadia Ego

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:16.580
Part I."

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.660
When La Sirena finally arrives at Soji's homeworld,
Soji reveals that the planet's synthetic inhabitants

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:23.940
call it "Coppelius."

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.680
This is almost assuredly a reference to the
19th century ballet Coppélia, in which a

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.860
young man falls in love with a life-size doll.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:36.019
In the ballet, the doll's inventor, Dr. Coppelius,
plans to sacrifice the young man in order

00:00:36.019 --> 00:00:37.780
to bring his creation to life.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.699
However, the young man's fiance manages to
save him by dressing up as the doll, and using

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.080
Coppelius' other creations as a distraction
so that the young couple can escape.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.170
The first part of the plot sounds eerily similar
to what Picard and his crew encounter on the

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.749
planet Coppelius, where Dr. Alton Inigo Soong
is willing to sacrifice the lives of all "organics"

00:00:57.749 --> 00:01:01.880
in order to ensure the continued existence
of his sentient synthetic creations.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.740
Shortly after La Sirena first arrives at Coppelius,
Narek's ship also emerges from the transwarp

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.240
conduit and immediately begins to attack.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.780
After trading shots back and forth, Narek's
ship suddenly seems to be experiencing some

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.500
sort of technical malfunction, with its cloaking
device glitching out and Narek's life signs

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.420
quickly fading.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.660
Picard orders Narek beamed aboard La Sirena,
but it turns out that it was all just a distraction.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.070
The ship they were seeing was actually a projection
of Narek's cloaking device.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.689
Really, his ship was behind La Sirena, and
he exploited their attempt at mercy to attack

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:34.380
them again.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.900
While the mechanics are different, Narek's
strategy is extremely reminiscent of the "Picard

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.540
Maneuver," a battle tactic Picard invented
while commanding the Stargazer that created

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.140
the illusion of a second ship.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.900
"And blowing into maximum warp speed, you
appeared, for an instant, to be in two places

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:50.480
at once."

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.680
"And our attacker fired on the wrong one."

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.539
Although Narek used his cloaking device to
create that illusion, both tactics involved

00:01:57.539 --> 00:02:01.750
confusing the opposing ship's sensors and
tricking them into firing on the illusion

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.040
instead of the real ship.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.860
After La Sirena crash lands on the surface
of Coppelius, the ship seems largely undamaged,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.700
except that none of the ship's automated systems
are up and running.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.920
This presents a problem for Agnes Jurati,
who is tasked with figuring out what's wrong

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.660
with Picard after the Admiral starts speaking
nonsensically on the bridge.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.020
Although the medical scanners aren't working,
Agnes lucks out when she finds an old med

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:24.520
kit.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.960
"I didn't know how I was going to run a scan
on you, and then I found this.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.000
An old-school medical tricorder."

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.320
Of course, medical tricorders are familiar
to anyone who has watched previous iterations

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:42.050
of Star Trek, but the most significant person
from Picard's past to use a medical tricorder

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:46.200
was Dr. Beverly Crusher, the Chief Medical
Officer on board the Enterprise.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.680
Dr. Crusher was also Picard's closest friend
and, in one version of the future, his wife,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:52.920
although they later got divorced.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.940
When the La Sirena crew ventures out onto
the surface of Coppelius, Raffi attributes

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.990
her nerves to the possibility of encountering
"angry reptiloids" or "homicidal fungi."

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:06.310
Neither of these is outside the realm of possibilities
on Star Trek.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:11.120
In the original series, the most notable encounter
with an angry reptiloid occurred in the episode

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.100
"Arena," in which Captain Kirk is told he
must fight to the death with the captain of

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:15.860
a Gorn ship.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.340
As for homicidal fungi, while no Star Trek
crew has yet had a run-in with murderous mushrooms,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.750
in the original series episode "The Conscience
of the King," the crew of the Enterprise encounters

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:30.940
the former governor of a colony responsible
for massacring 4,000 of his colonists after

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.680
an aggressive strain of exotic fungi wiped
out most of their food stores.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.850
The governor's justification for his homicidal
actions was that he was trying to ensure that

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.460
at least some of his people would live, even
if it meant sacrificing many of them.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.860
This is not too far off from the logic presented
by Sutra in "Et in Arcadia Ego, Part I," in

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:52.590
which she argues that wiping out all organic
life in the universe is a worthwhile tradeoff

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.980
if it ensures that the synthetic race will
live on.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.300
After arriving on Coppelius, the long-range
sensors on the crashed Borg cube confirm that

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.060
a fleet of Romulan warbirds is indeed on its
way to the planet, and will arrive uncomfortably

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:05.560
soon.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:11.240
Raffi tells Rios that in total, there are
218 warbirds approaching, although Raffi jokes

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.740
that they really only have to worry about
the first 109.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:17.640
While it's always hard to say for sure whether
numbers like these are supposed to be a reference

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:22.870
to anything at all, 218 could be a nod to
the fifth season Next Generation episode "Cause

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.980
and Effect," which was given production code
218.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.120
In the episode, the Enterprise gets caught
in a time loop, and only manages to escape

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.770
once Data figures out a way to send a message
to himself through time, giving him the information

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:35.800
he needs to break the loop.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:41.680
In "Et in Arcadia Ego, Part I," Sutra realizes
that the Admonition is a message being sent

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.350
from an ancient race of synthetics to the
synthetic lifeforms of the future.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:49.650
Keeping with the idea of production codes,
the Next Generation episode "Justice" was

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:54.500
given the code 109, and sees Picard facing
off against a powerful and highly advanced

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.520
being who watches over a race of less advanced
people.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:02.800
In "Justice," Picard opposes the death sentence
given to an innocent member of his crew, arguing

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:07.930
that there is nothing just about such an extreme
unilateral punishment that doesn't take individual

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:12.460
circumstances into account - a similar argument
to the one we see him attempt to make at the

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.720
end of "Et in Arcadia Ego, Part I."

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.400
When Picard and the crew of La Sirena arrive
in Coppelius Station, we get a brief glimpse

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.060
of two synthetics playing a game that looks
very similar to the 3D chess that has been

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.380
seen on several other versions of Star Trek.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.670
One episode of The Next Generation in which
3D chess played a prominent role was in a

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:36.270
third season episode in which Will Riker uses
his captors' game to maneuver his way out

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.160
of his cell after he and Deanna Troi are taken
hostage.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.420
Picard will likely have to employ similar
levels of cunning and strategic thinking in

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:48.090
order to escape the circumstances he finds
himself in at the end of "Et in Arcadia Ego,

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:48.720
Part I."

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.640
When the golden-skinned synthetic Sutra is
introduced, Dr. Soong mentions that she is

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:58.960
extremely interested in Vulcan culture, saying
that she has read Surak and "plays the ka'athyra

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.580
beautifully," and has even taught herself
how to perform a Vulcan mind meld.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.280
Surak was the revered Vulcan philosopher who
helped shape Vulcan culture with his teachings

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.320
on peace and logic.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.100
Meanwhile, the ka'athyra was a form of Vulcan
lute.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.640
"It is a five stringed instrument, tuned to
a diatonic scale.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.240
It can be very soothing."

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.650
Several characters have played the ka'athyra
on past iterations of Star Trek, including

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.220
Spock and Uhura on the original series, and
Tuvok on Voyager.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.460
One of the cuddliest recurring characters
on The Next Generation was Data's ginger housecat,

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.550
Spot, who appeared in numerous episodes.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.900
Caring for Spot and bonding with the cat was
a pivotal part of Data's ongoing quest to

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.090
become more human.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:39.920
"And you must talk to him.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:42.800
Tell him he is a pretty cat.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.140
And a good cat."

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.460
In "Et in Arcadia Ego, Part I," Agnes is seen
cuddling a remarkably familiar ginger housecat,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.120
whom she confirms is even named, "Spot II."

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.700
But don't get too excited about Spot's family
tree continuing on through 2399; this version

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:03.840
of Spot is actually a synthetic, created by
Soong and seemingly modeled after the original

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:04.340
Spot.

